hat is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186385872 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3b433b7-383a-5380-9fae-9ee33c84e8d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385872 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-I=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 73\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 72\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186385873 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385873 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385873 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385873 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385873 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385873 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385873 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385873 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186385874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218638587