8149 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186418136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418149 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418149 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418149 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186418104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418149 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cb9ef2be-f5d0-5af1-b53b-519b082df85b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\,∗\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70b4f245-e7d8-5908-9e68-f829b0901af8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418156 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96305" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9020d5f8-c5f3-54dc-9474-2d2b14ec7c2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418157 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186428016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186428017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186428018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186428019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186428020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1045 "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186428020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428008 #edb/re