78 #edb/ref 17592186428010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428014 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "01f21526-a5f6-5403-8d94-60d82378f21e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186428014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428015 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}73 & 24 \\\\ 24 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186428015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39a1a8bd-f69f-5f36-99bc-5bd95f9b782d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428015 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=9.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=81.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-81I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-81I=\\begin{bmatrix}73 & 24 \\\\ 24 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-81\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 24 \\\\ 24 & -72\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-81I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sq